sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: docs/reference/tasks.md#check-an-external-group, the shape guide (official `@swamp/ssh exec`), the publish chart for the handoff push.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rule 22 (no shipped scripts: every task VM step is one `@swamp/ssh exec` argv, 209 shapes the outputs in TypeScript; no step copies a script to the VM), rule 16 (no snapshot after the push: the import checks the bundled commit and tree).
  %% status: migrated
  %% command: task:check
  %% kind: tasks.check
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane I icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Task VM<br/>reached over ssh
  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repo · Checks
  participant I as Instance<br/>via its Node
  CLI->>GW: task:check 7<br/> --phase=handoff
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit task:check 7 --phase=handoff`
  %%> - syntax: `<task> --phase=baseline|handoff [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  Note over GW: Admit the check<br/>check 17 · task #5170
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - group 42 is open and external, with its own task copy, else `tasks.no_workspace` ("The group requires its task copy."): task VM workspaces are removed
  %%> - subtask 7 is the current open subtask, else `tasks.subtask_not_current`
  %%> - a baseline only before any implementer started, else `tasks.baseline_done`
  %%> - `task:start` of a Project whose default is a container, or of a nested-fleet Project, creates that ownership with a task copy
  %%> - that workspace's last completion did not fail, else `tasks.copy_completion_failed` with its Operation, failed step, error code and the retry `orbit instance:complete ID`; and it is `active`, else `tasks.copy_not_ready`
  %%> - that workspace needs a running runtime, else `tasks.instance_not_running`, and a recorded branch, else `tasks.no_workspace`
  %%> - that workspace refuses a handoff that needs hosted CI (`tasks.instance_hosted_check_unavailable`), before a check row exists
  %%> - its baseline has an empty setup list: the copied seed already ran the Project's setup steps
  %%> Result:
  %%> - check 17 recorded as running; the full intent holds the Project check, setup (baseline), deliverables (handoff), base commit and the VM's SSH target
  %%> - a container workspace adds `workspace`: the recorded Incus project and container, the Instance branch and a new scratch ID; the SSH target is its Node
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-check
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the intent, and vault key names for the CI tokens when the Project requires handoff CI
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5170
  opt recorded container Instance workspace
    SW->>I: Check in the Instance
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one child workflow runs the whole check through Ansible phases on the Instance Node; every repository command runs in the container as `agent` in `/srv/workspace`, with the agent's HOME and login shell
    %%> - the task VM steps below are skipped; the same TypeScript rules judge the evidence, so outcomes match
    %%> - a scoped Project check such as `bin/task-check` prints one summary line per part; its `ci-policy` part runs the CI policy tests (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tools -p 'test_ci_*.py'`) when the change touches what those tests read: the CI registry and its fixture tomls, the workflows, the CI scripts or a Swamp test file, before Pest and the fixtures, within the check's budget
    %%> - see [task Instance workspace](/reference/task-instance-workspace#check-phases)
    %%> Base first (handoff only):
    %%> - its child first runs `task-copy-base`, phase `base`, the same one `task:sync` runs ([Bring main into a copy](/reference/tasks#bring-main-into-a-copy)): the Node fetches the current default branch head into the copy's mirror and the copy gets it in `refs/remotes/origin/<default>`
    %%> - the read token follows the sync's rules: the Gateway mints it as the run starts, the Node's one fetch reads it as a Git header through stdin, and it never reaches the copy
    %%> - a failed base (mirror or GitHub down) does not block the check: the check runs on the tree as it is, and the result says `base: unavailable` with `base_reason`; the base's scratch cleanup runs after it, whatever it did
    %%> - then the `base-merge` phase, as `agent` in the copy: `git merge-tree --write-tree HEAD refs/remotes/origin/<default>`; HEAD already holds the head: nothing, `merged_default` is null
    %%> - a clean merge: any uncommitted work is committed first with the check's message, then one merge commit, parents HEAD then the head, message `Merge main <full sha> before the check`, author and committer `Orbit <orbit@users.noreply.github.com>`
    %%> - only the copy's own workflow changes refuse at publish (`git diff <merge-base>...<commit>`): the default branch's workflow changes that the merge brings in are never the copy's
    %%> - a conflict refuses with `tasks.check_conflict` and `conflicting_paths`, at most 100 in at most 3,000 bytes, and `conflicting_more`, before the long parts run; the workspace is left exactly as it was
    %%> - every refusal comes before the copy changes: the work and merge commits are objects no ref names; a merge that would add a file over an untracked or ignored one fails with `tasks.workspace_phase_failed`; only then the working tree, the index and the branch move
    %%> - after the merge step succeeds, HEAD holds the default head, whether this check merged it or an earlier check, or a sync and a hand merge, did; file deliverables and `fails_on_base` runs compare with the subtask's start plus that head, so main's changes are never the subtask's work
    %%> - the base-merge, check, commit and commit-ref phases run as the behavior `task-instance-check-NODE-GROUP`, for up to 900 s, 10,800 s, 600 s and 600 s, so the check of another group's copy on this Node never waits for its lock; its short steps keep their Node names
    %%> Refuses:
    %%> - a changed checkout identity or branch: `tasks.workspace_identity_changed`
  end
  Note over SW: Build every argv
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - TypeScript on 209 builds each step's `@swamp/ssh exec` argv from the intent
  %%> Refuses:
  %%> - an absolute or `..` deliverable directory, a non-canonical overlay path, or a `fails_on_base` command without a base commit: `tasks.deliverable_invalid`, before any command
  opt baseline with setup steps
    SW->>N: Run setup in order
    %%> How:
    %%> - each is `env -C <checkout> … /bin/bash -lc <command>` with its own timeout; `@orbit/ci-caches` expands to the shared program
    %%> - every command writes its output to `<run>/output/<step>` on the VM, never to the SSH session, so native history keeps only its exit code
    %%> - a failed step skips the rest and the Project check
  end
  SW->>N: Run the Project check
  %%> How:
  %%> - one argv in the checkout; its output goes to its file on the VM, not to native history
  %%> - its formatters run in fix mode, so their edits belong to the checked tree
  SW->>N: Snapshot the workspace
  %%> How:
  %%> - `git read-tree HEAD`, `git add --all`, `git write-tree` on the run's index file, and `git rev-parse HEAD`; the real index is untouched
  opt handoff with deliverables
    SW->>N: Run each deliverable
    %%> Rules:
    %%> - a failed setup step or Project check skips every command deliverable
    %%> How:
    %%> - `diff`: `git diff-tree -r --no-renames --name-status -z <base> <tree>` for file deliverables
    %%> - a `fails_on_base` command first runs on its start commit: `git archive`, `tar -x`, a copy of installed `vendor` and `node_modules`, the declared test paths, then the command
    %%> - a command whose last simple command calls Pest gets CI's `--default-time-limit`, which the Project's check command prints as `ci-time-limit: N` or `ci-time-limit: none` for `--ci-time-limit <directory>`, asked only when it is one executable path whose file contains the text `--ci-time-limit` (any other check command is never run for it and the command runs as given); its start-commit run gets the same limit (docs/reference/tasks.md#a-pest-command-gets-cis-time-limit)
    %%> Refuses:
    %%> - a missing overlay file or directory: `tasks.deliverable_invalid`
  end
  SW->>N: Snapshot the checked tree
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - a moved tree makes the check `changed`; a failed command or unmet deliverable makes it `failed`
  %%> - a command deliverable with no run, because setup or the Project check failed first or no evidence was recorded, reads `not_run` with that reason; it also makes the check `failed`
  %%> How:
  %%> - official SSH copy downloads the private command files; the local reader redacts them, keeps a 16 KiB tail and deletes them before history
  %%> - the output ends with a `Deliverables` block and one line that states the outcome; a later `changed` or CI failure restates that line (docs/reference/tasks.md#stored-result)
  opt passed handoff
    SW->>N: Commit the candidate
    %%> How:
    %%> - `git add --all`, one `git commit` as Orbit with hooks off, `git rev-parse HEAD HEAD^{tree} HEAD^`
    %%> - in a container workspace, a clean `HEAD` that `refs/orbit/check-operations/` already names makes no commit: the candidate is that recorded `HEAD`, so a cancel finds no empty commit; it reads `git rev-parse HEAD HEAD^{tree}`. Any other tree gets a fresh commit with the cleaned message, `--allow-empty`
    %%> - 209 checks the tree and parent equal the checked snapshot, or, for a container workspace only, that the candidate is the checked `HEAD` with its tree, else `changed`; then `git update-ref refs/orbit/check-operations/<operation>`
  end
  opt Project requires handoff CI
    SW->>GH: Push the candidate
    %%> How:
    %%> - export, import, mint, push and revoke as in `task:publish`; the run then ends with CI pending
    %%> - a native schedule reads the Checks once a minute, snapshots the workspace again and settles the check
  end
  Note over SW: Read the result
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the receipt of `Push the candidate`, or of `Check in the Instance` for a container workspace
  %%> - a direct data step exposes the push producer's result JSON to a calling workflow; Swamp propagates only one level of child behavior outputs
  %%> - it keeps the complete check receipt on passed, failed and changed outcomes
  Note over SW: Check the result
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - native assert checks the push producer receipt for an execution error
  %%> - the Gateway captures that receipt in the same history read; no shell prints it again
  %%> - a container workspace's receipt comes from `Check in the Instance` in the same shape
  opt hosted CI is pending
    Note over GW: Record scheduled CI
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - on each native schedule tick, `collect` shapes public observations for admitted checks after their SSH snapshots
    %%> - queued or running GitHub Actions jobs on the candidate keep CI pending even before Required checks appears
    %%> - another head or App cannot extend the wait; missing CI still expires after ten minutes, active CI after 55 minutes
    %%> - the existing reader takes these rows directly from the collector output; no reporting shell runs
    %%> - Swamp inlines only a small step output in the run history; a larger one, such as a check with long output, is read as the `output` data artifact of `task-check-ci-data` with `data.get`, at the version the history names
  end
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>outcome · commit · ci
    %%> Result:
    %%> - a copy's handoff check also names `default_commit`, the default head it judged against, `merged_default`, the head it merged or null, and `base`: `current`, or `unavailable` with `base_reason`
    Note over GW: Record check 17<br/>passed, failed or changed
    %%> Rules:
    %%> - a check preserves its admitted subtask base as the start commit; the candidate HEAD is never a new base for a retry
    %%> - a passed handoff check records the group's pull request mode in effect at that moment (`pr_mode`); only a check that recorded `pr_when_done` lets the `task:pr-when-done` tick publish by itself. Recording it starts nothing (docs/reference/tasks.md#where-the-mode-comes-from)
  else a step fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or no reply
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    Note over GW: Record the failed check<br/>group lifecycle stays external
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5170
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
  end
